The CGPs were characterized by their IR spectra, elemental analysis, $\mathbf{X}$ -ray powder diffraction (XRPD), and thermogravimetric (TG) analyses. The IR spectra showed a stretching band $(2870-2920\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) derived from the $-(\mathrm{CH}_{2})-$ group in $\mathbf{L}$ , which increases with the increasing L content. The absorption derived from the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-O-C}}$ stretching vibration $(1020{-}1150\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ for the free $\upgamma\mathrm{-CD}$ was a sharp peak, while that of the immobilized $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ was broad because of the cross-linking (Figure S2).[22] Elemental analysis revealed that one unit of CD-MOF was modified with 16.02 molecules of L. These results confirmed that $\mathbf{L}$ reacted in the nanopores of the CD-MOF and acted as a cross-linker between the CDs in the CD-MOF. To study the crystallinity of CL-CD-MOF and CGP, we performed XRPD analysis. As shown in Figure 4 a, the XRPD patterns illustrated that CLCD-MOF was a crystalline material similar to CD-MOF. For CGP, no apparent diffraction peaks were observed, indicating that CL-CD-MOF became amorphous after swelling, like isoreticular MOF (IRMOF) by the click reaction with external cross-linkers.[20] The cross-linking reaction proceeded between the epoxy group of L and the hydroxy groups of each $\gamma\mathrm{-CD}$ in the CD-MOF pores. Cross-linking between the organic linkers provided an insoluble polymer network, whereas imperfections in the cross-linking did not allow for a crystalline organic network polymer similar to a covalent organic framework (COF) to form,[23] but instead gave amorphous polymer networks that can swell by soaking in the solvent.
CD-MOF-Micro1, CD-MOF-Micro2, and CD-MOFNano were cross-linked by a similar procedure, but differed in their isolation of the product by centrifugation of the precipitate. The XRPD patterns showed that the cross-linking reaction proceeded while retaining the crystal shape, and CGP-Micro1, CGP-Micro2, and CGP-Nano were amorphous (Figure S4, S5). CGP, CGP-Micro1, CGP-Micro2, and CGPNano were insoluble after treatment in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ SEM images showed that CGP-Micro1, CGP-Micro2, and CGP-Nano retained their cubic shapes as shown in Figure 5. This indicated that a similar cross-linking reaction occurred a polymer gel (Figure 4 a). Furthermore, the thermal stabilities and guest inclusion properties of CD-MOF, CL-CDMOF, and CGP after being immersed in EtOH or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ were estimated by TG analysis as shown in Figure $4\mathsf{b}.$ . The pyrolysis temperatures of CD-MOF, CL-CD-MOF, and CGP were determined to be $298.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , $325.5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , and $356.4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ respectively (Figure S3 and Table S1). The thermal stability was improved by cross-linking the $\upgamma\mathrm{-CD}$ units. For evaluation of the inclusion abilities of CD-MOF, CL-CD-MOF, and CGP, we estimated the weight loss upon heating as the vaporization of included EtOH. The number of included EtOH molecules per unit structure was determined to be 91.0 for CD-MOF and 7.4 for CL-CD-MOF. This suggested that the nanopores of CL-CD-MOF were mostly filled with reacted L following the cross-linking reaction. Besides, CGP could include 16.1 EtOH molecules and $263.8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules per unit. For EtOH, the amount of the guest included in the CGP slightly increased compared to the CL-CD-MOF owing to a slight expansion of the network constructed from the $\upgamma\cdot$ - CD and L. Moreover, the entrapment of a large amount of water in the CGP was attributed to swelling of the CGP because of its good compatibility with water. The degree of swelling was estimated from the change in size and it agreed with this value. These results indicated that the cross-linking reaction of L induced the network formation. These behaviors were similar to those of the cross-linking of the azide-modifed between the epoxy group of $\mathbf{L}$ and the hydroxy group of each $\upgamma\mathrm{-CD}$ in the micro- and nanosized CD-MOF pores. We found from the SEM images that the average size of the CDMOF-Nano and CGP-Nano cubes was around $338\pm76\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $328\pm80\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Figure S6). These results demonstrated that the CGP-Micro1, CGP-Micro2, and CGP-Nano were successfully prepared with the same size and shape as the corresponding CD-MOF (Figure S6 and Table S2). Therefore, the size variation of the CD-MOFs from nanometers to millimeters enabled us to control the size and shape of the CGP.
In summary, we fabricated uniform cubic gel particles with well-defined edges and square faces using internal crosslinking of the CD-MOF crystals followed by loss of coordinating metal ions. The cubic gel particles retained the shape and size of the original CD-MOF crystals, indicating that by controlling the recrystallization conditions a wide range of sizes of CGPs, from millimeters to nanometers, can be produced. Moreover, using MOF crystals as templates for the CGPs should enable us to prepare a wide variety of polyhedral gel particles from the MOF crystals with controlled polyhedral shapes. Therefore, this method can open a new horizon for the preparation of micro- and nanosized polyhedral polymer gels with well-defined shapes and sizes. These gels could provide mesoscopic building blocks with a soft interface for constructing complex architectures by selforganization, or be used in biomedical applications such as drug carriers and cell-support materials. Studies of the internal cross-linking of MOF crystals with other organic linkers and metal ions to produce polyhedral gel particles with shapes other than spheres and cubes are currently ongoing.
## Experimental Section
Preparation of CD-MOF, CD-MOF-Micro, and CD-MOF-Nano: $\gamma\mathrm{-CD}$ ( $163\mathrm{mg}$ , $0.126\mathrm{mmol}$ ) was dissolved in aq. KOH ( $200\mathrm{m}\mathrm{\bfM}$ , $5.0\mathrm{mL}$ ) in a glass tube. The solution was filtered through a $13\mathrm{mm}$ syringe filter ( $0.45\upmu\mathrm{m}$ PTFE membrane) into a glass tube. MeOH was allowed to vapor-diffuse into the solution at the temperature $(25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ of the first crystal-growth time. $5\mathrm{mL}$ of the solution was transferred to another glass tube containing CTAB $(40\mathrm{mg},0.110\mathrm{mmol})$ . When the CTAB had thoroughly dissolved, the solution was incubated at the temperature $(25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ of the second crystal-growth time. A tube of the turbid solution $(500\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was centrifuged at $500\mathrm{rpm}$ for 3 min and the supernatant was removed. CD-MOF-Micro and CD-MOF-Nano were redispersed and washed in $1.0\mathrm{mL}$ of EtOH.
Preparation of CL-CD-MOF: Ethylene glycol diglycidyl ether/ EtOH solution (5 mL, 1.5m) containing CD-MOFs were heated to $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an oven for 3 days. The solution was added to a tube, centrifuged at $500\mathrm{rpm}$ for $3\mathrm{min}$ and the supernatant removed. CLCD-MOFs were obtained and repeatedly washed with EtOH.
Preparation of CGP: CL-CD-MOF was immersed in an excess of mixed solvent $\mathrm{\mathrm{EtOH/H_{2}O}}=1{:}1$ $(\nu/\nu)$ ) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , sequentially.
